From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pet0203 <Pet0203@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vboot: run the disk unlock when the kernel command line asks
 for it

Call the unlock from vboot_boot_kernel() when the selected kernel's command
line contains "dm-mod.create=".

dm-mod.create= is how a root device gets mapped without an initramfs, and a
command line that has it belongs to a kernel that will go looking for
":64:logon:" keys only the firmware can supply. The command line is inside
the signed kernel partition, so what decides whether a PIN is asked for is
covered by the same signature as the kernel itself: nothing on the disk,
and nobody at the keyboard, can turn the prompt on or off.

It also means the unlock is inert until such a kernel is installed. A
kernel that says nothing about dm (a rescue image, for instance) boots as
before, with no prompt and with PCR12 left alone.

Developer and recovery mode are excluded. PCR0 differs in both, so the TPM
would refuse the read whatever was typed, and a prompt there could only
collect a PIN it cannot use.

A failed unlock does not stop the boot. The kernel then fails to map root
and says so, which is a better place to find out than a firmware that
refuses to go on. Use rootwait=<seconds> and panic=<seconds> on the command
line so that this ends in a reboot rather than a kernel waiting forever on
a blank screen.
---
 src/vboot/stages.c | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vboot/stages.c b/src/vboot/stages.c
index 83cf89c..2a664fa 100644
--- a/src/vboot/stages.c
+++ b/src/vboot/stages.c
@@ -38,6 +38,7 @@
 #include "vboot/nvdata.h"
 #include "vboot/secdata_tpm.h"
 #include "vboot/stages.h"
+#include "vboot/unlock.h"
 #include "vboot/util/commonparams.h"
 #include "vboot/util/flag.h"
 #include "vboot/util/memory.h"
@@ -226,6 +227,25 @@ void vboot_boot_kernel(VbSelectAndLoadKernelParams *kparams)
 		bi.cmd_line = cmd_line_buf;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A kernel that maps its root through dm needs the volume keys, and
+	 * only the firmware can get them out of the Cr50. Ask for the PIN and
+	 * hand them over. A kernel that says nothing about dm -- a rescue
+	 * image, or anything from before the disk was encrypted -- is
+	 * booted exactly as before, with no prompt.
+	 *
+	 * Not in developer or recovery mode: PCR0 differs there, so the TPM
+	 * would refuse the read whatever was typed, and all a prompt could do
+	 * is take a PIN it cannot use.
+	 *
+	 * The return value is deliberately ignored. If the keys did not come
+	 * out, the kernel fails to map root and says so, which is a better
+	 * place to find out than a firmware that refuses to boot at all.
+	 */
+	if (bi.cmd_line && strstr(bi.cmd_line, "dm-mod.create=") &&
+	    !vboot_in_developer() && !vboot_in_recovery())
+		unlock_disk_keys(UNLOCK_NV_INDEX, UNLOCK_NV_KEY_COUNT);
+
 	if (crossystem_setup(FIRMWARE_TYPE_AUTO_DETECT))
 		return;
 
